\section{Phase-Strategy Crossover Details}
\label{app:crossover-details}

At $s{=}0.25$, a strategy ranking reversal emerges (Figure~\ref{fig:crossover}): importance-based eviction yields \emph{lower} II than random in the high-sparsity phase ($0.032$ vs.\ $0.818$) but \emph{higher} II in the low-sparsity phase ($7.196$ vs.\ $1.363$---a $5.3{\times}$ reversal). This crossover is present at all four sparsity levels, with reversal magnitude of $3.9{\times}$ at $s{=}0.15$, $5.4{\times}$ at $s{=}0.20$, $5.3{\times}$ at $s{=}0.25$, and $3.6{\times}$ at $s{=}0.30$ (as random eviction severity also rises). The reversal arises because head pruning redistributes attention mass, altering importance rankings in a way that couples the two sparsity mechanisms in low-sparsity phases (\S\ref{sec:discrimination-collapse}). % exp_id: bf16_importance_s025, bf16_ds_rand_s025; source: experiment_summary_table.json; 7.196/1.363=5.28

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_crossover.pdf}
\caption{Strategy-dependent crossover at $s{=}0.25$. Grouped bar chart of median II by eviction strategy and reasoning phase. Importance-based eviction outperforms random in the high-sparsity phase but produces $5.3{\times}$ higher II in the low-sparsity phase, reversing the strategy ranking.}
\label{fig:crossover}
\end{figure}


\section{Cross-Model Replication Details}
\label{app:cross-model-details}

Both models share the Qwen2ForCausalLM backbone (28 layers, 28 heads, 4-group GQA) but differ in training: DeepSeek uses reasoning distillation from DeepSeek-R1, while Qwen2.5 uses standard instruction tuning without explicit reasoning delimiters. This controlled comparison isolates the effect of training methodology on the compound sparsity interaction.

Despite lacking explicit \texttt{<think>} delimiters, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct exhibits sufficient variation in attention sparsity to permit phase segmentation via the same quantile-based method (\S\ref{sec:phase-segmentation}). Four of the 50 problems are excluded due to degenerate phase assignments, yielding $n{=}46$ valid problems for Qwen (vs.\ $n{=}49$ for DeepSeek).

Table~\ref{tab:cross-model} reports the cross-model comparison. Qwen reproduces the monotonic severity gradient at ${\sim}1/5$ of DeepSeek's magnitude. Figure~\ref{fig:cross-model} visualizes this comparison. % exp_id: cross_model_analysis.json; cohens_d_bf16_qwen.json

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model comparison of median compound severity difference ($\Delta$) under importance-based eviction.}
\label{tab:cross-model}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Model & $s{=}0.15$ & $s{=}0.20$ & $s{=}0.25$ & $s{=}0.30$ \\
\midrule
DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B & 0.39 & 1.48 & 7.16 & 14.59 \\ % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030
Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct         & 0.04 & 0.23 & 1.27 & 2.94 \\ % exp_id: bf16_qw_imp_s015..s030
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_crossmodel.pdf}
\caption{Cross-model comparison of the median severity difference under importance-based eviction. Both DeepSeek (solid) and Qwen (dashed) exhibit monotonically increasing $\Delta$ with sparsity; Qwen's magnitude is approximately one-fifth of DeepSeek's.} % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030, bf16_qw_imp_s015..s030
\label{fig:cross-model}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Per-problem concordance.}
The per-problem concordance across models is not significant (mean $\rho = -0.11$, $p = 0.926$), indicating that while the aggregate gradient direction replicates, the specific problems driving severity differ between models---consistent with model-specific attention patterns rather than a universal per-problem vulnerability ranking. % exp_id: cross_model_analysis.json (spearman_rho=1.0, gradient_permutation_p=0.043, per_problem_concordance_p=0.926, mean_rho=-0.107)

\paragraph{Random eviction control.}
As a control, random eviction on Qwen yields no systematic phase gradient at three of four sparsity levels (3/4 FAIL), mirroring DeepSeek's 4/4 FAIL pattern. At $s{=}0.30$, Qwen random produces a borderline PASS (permutation $p = 0.012$) but with negligible effect size ($d_z = -0.04$, CI spans zero), indicating no meaningful phase modulation. % exp_id: bf16_qw_rand_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json


\section{Permutation Test Details}
\label{app:permutation-details}

For each problem $i$ and sparsity level $s$, we compute the compound severity difference $\Delta_i^{(\text{strat})} = \mathrm{II}_{\textit{low},i} - \mathrm{II}_{\textit{high},i}$ under each eviction strategy. The test statistic is the median of the paired difference $\widetilde{\Delta} = \text{median}_i\!\bigl(\Delta_i^{(\text{imp})} - \Delta_i^{(\text{rand})}\bigr)$, measuring whether importance-based eviction produces systematically larger phase-dependent severity than random eviction. We permute strategy labels $10{,}000$ times to construct the null distribution and apply Bonferroni correction for 4 sparsity-level comparisons.

Per-problem distributions of the paired severity difference $\Delta_i = \mathrm{II}_{\textit{low},i} - \mathrm{II}_{\textit{high},i}$ under importance-based eviction are right-skewed at all sparsity levels, with mean/median ratios ranging from $1.2{\times}$ ($s{=}0.15$) to $1.6{\times}$ ($s{=}0.30$) for DeepSeek and $1.1{\times}$ to $1.4{\times}$ for Qwen.

The permutation null distributions are approximately symmetric around zero at all sparsity levels. At $s{=}0.15$, the observed statistic falls at the $99.68$th percentile of the null ($p_{\text{Bonf}} = 0.0032$); at $s \geq 0.20$, no permutation exceeds the observed statistic.

Table~\ref{tab:permutation} reports the results. All four sparsity levels reject $H_0$ after Bonferroni correction ($p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.005$), with observed median differences increasing monotonically from $0.37$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $9.85$ at $s{=}0.30$. The monotonic growth of both the point estimate and the CI lower bound ($0.19 \to 0.69 \to 1.66 \to 6.70$) confirms that compound severity amplification strengthens with sparsity---not merely in central tendency but also in worst-case lower bounds. Figure~\ref{fig:permtest-forest} presents these results as a forest plot. % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest; source: permutation_test_results.json

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Paired permutation test for compound severity: importance vs.\ random eviction on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B ($n{=}49$, $10{,}000$ permutations, Bonferroni-corrected).}
\label{tab:permutation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}rrcl@{}}
\toprule
$s$ & Observed $\widetilde{\Delta}$ & 95\% CI & $p_{\text{Bonf}}$ \\
\midrule
0.15 & 0.37 & $[0.19,\; 0.62]$ & 0.0032 \\ % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest_s015
0.20 & 0.97 & $[0.69,\; 1.49]$ & ${<}\,0.001$ \\ % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest_s020
0.25 & 3.34 & $[1.66,\; 6.64]$ & ${<}\,0.001$ \\ % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest_s025
0.30 & 9.85 & $[6.70,\; 16.29]$ & ${<}\,0.001$ \\ % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest_s030
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_bootstrap.pdf}
\caption{Forest plot of permutation test results. Horizontal bars show 95\% permutation CIs; diamonds mark observed median differences $\widetilde{\Delta}$. All CIs lie entirely above zero, confirming significant strategy-dependent severity at every sparsity level.}
\label{fig:permtest-forest}
\end{figure}


\section{Analysis: Entropy-Discrimination Collapse}
\label{app:analysis}

\subsection{Discrimination Collapse}
\label{sec:discrimination-collapse}

Head pruning reduces the number of heads contributing to cumulative importance scores, flattening the score distribution. Table~\ref{tab:discrimination-metrics-full} quantifies this effect: the Gini coefficient decreases by $0.030$--$0.081$ (DeepSeek) and $0.010$--$0.020$ (Qwen) after pruning, with consistent negative deltas across all 16 conditions (both models $\times$ 4 sparsity levels $\times$ 2 strategies). The CV delta is likewise negative in all 8 importance-based conditions. The same pattern holds under random eviction, confirming that discrimination collapse is a consequence of head pruning \emph{per se}, not of the eviction strategy. % exp_id: c4_entropy_deepseek, c4_entropy_qwen

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Discrimination collapse under importance-based eviction. $\Delta\mathrm{Gini}$ and $\Delta\mathrm{CV}$ are post-pruning minus baseline; negative values indicate reduced discriminative power.}
\label{tab:discrimination-metrics-full}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrr}
\toprule
Model & $s$ & $\Delta\mathrm{Gini}$ & $\Delta\mathrm{CV}$ \\
\midrule
DeepSeek & $0.15$ & $-0.030$ & $-0.56$ \\
DeepSeek & $0.20$ & $-0.040$ & $-0.77$ \\
DeepSeek & $0.25$ & $-0.065$ & $-1.29$ \\
DeepSeek & $0.30$ & $-0.081$ & $-1.66$ \\
\midrule
Qwen & $0.15$ & $-0.010$ & $-0.05$ \\
Qwen & $0.20$ & $-0.012$ & $-0.21$ \\
Qwen & $0.25$ & $-0.010$ & $-1.49$ \\
Qwen & $0.30$ & $-0.020$ & $-1.14$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

When importance scores lose discriminability, thresholding degenerates into noise-sensitive selection---functionally approaching biased random eviction. This is phase-dependent: in low-sparsity phases, attention is already broadly distributed, so pruning-induced flattening has maximum impact; in high-sparsity phases, concentration is preserved. Random eviction is immune because its decisions are independent of score quality, explaining $G \approx 1$. % exp_id: c4_entropy_deepseek, c4_entropy_qwen

\subsection{Entropy Redistribution}
\label{sec:entropy-redistribution}

Beyond discrimination collapse, we examine whether head pruning also redistributes the attention entropy of surviving heads. We measure the mean attention entropy per head:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{H}_i^{(h)} = -\sum_{j=1}^{T} a_{ij}^{(h)} \log a_{ij}^{(h)}
    \label{eq:attn-entropy}
\end{equation}
and compute $\Delta\mathcal{H}$ as the change from baseline (no pruning) to the pruned condition.

For DeepSeek under importance-based eviction, the entropy delta is consistently negative across all four sparsity levels ($\Delta\mathcal{H} \in [-0.040, -0.047]$; Table~\ref{tab:entropy-deltas}), indicating that importance-based pruning systematically selects heads whose removal concentrates attention in the surviving heads, reinforcing discrimination collapse. % exp_id: c4_entropy_deepseek

For Qwen, the entropy pattern only partially replicates. The delta is negative at $s{=}0.15$ ($\Delta\mathcal{H} = -0.073$) and $s{=}0.20$ ($-0.076$), consistent with DeepSeek. However, at $s{=}0.25$, the delta reverses to $+0.045$, with 22 of 30 problems showing positive entropy change. At $s{=}0.30$, the delta is near zero ($-0.007$). The $s{=}0.25$ reversal is systematic rather than a statistical artifact: it is consistent across all three reasoning phases (low: $+0.042$, mid: $+0.034$, high: $+0.017$). We interpret this as a genuine model-specific difference, possibly arising from distinct head specialization patterns: Qwen's instruction tuning (vs.\ DeepSeek's distillation-based training) may produce head configurations where removing 25\% of heads triggers compensatory broadening rather than concentration. % exp_id: c4_entropy_qwen

We characterize discrimination collapse as the robust, model-independent driver of the phase-strategy interaction, with entropy redistribution as a secondary amplifier that is consistent in DeepSeek but model-dependent in Qwen.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Attention entropy deltas ($\Delta\mathcal{H}$) under importance-based eviction. DeepSeek shows consistent entropy compression; Qwen partially replicates with a reversal at $s{=}0.25$.}
\label{tab:entropy-deltas}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llr}
\toprule
Model & $s$ & $\Delta\mathcal{H}$ \\
\midrule
DeepSeek & $0.15$ & $-0.040$ \\
DeepSeek & $0.20$ & $-0.047$ \\
DeepSeek & $0.25$ & $-0.045$ \\
DeepSeek & $0.30$ & $-0.044$ \\
\midrule
Qwen & $0.15$ & $-0.073$ \\
Qwen & $0.20$ & $-0.076$ \\
Qwen & $0.25$ & $+0.045$ \\
Qwen & $0.30$ & $-0.007$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

